le  to run for office so I think it's both a  separate issue as well as something that  works in tandem with all these other  reforms and kind of in parallel with  that I'm curious whether you see the way  in which at least at the presidential  level we have this electoral college  system I mean is that something that you  think plays into the reforms you're  talking about or or is that more of a  separate issue  well I think the electoral college plays  into the voter apathy concern that  involves so many of our elections and  you know one reason that people don't  vote is they feel like it's not gonna  make a difference and why bother I mean  you know if you're a Republican in  California or a Democrat in Texas you  think that you know the result is  essentially preordained in the electoral  college but that said there's also a  reform possible for the electoral  college itself that I talked about  briefly towards the end of the book and  this is the national popular vote  interstate compact for essentially  states are agreeing that instead of  awarding their electoral college votes  to whoever wins their state they'll  award it to the national popular vote  winner and that's really gaining steam  as well yep that we've actually talked  about that quite a bit and of course  until there are enough states whose sum  total of electors totals 270 it doesn't  get triggered but a number of states and  I don't remember it but it for some  reason I think it's 12 or 13 is that  right I've said it's maybe 12 plus DC  now that's a very interesting initiative  which we will we'll keep talking about  we've been speaking with Josh Douglas  who's a law professor at the University  of Kentucky about his book vote for us  how to take back our elections and  change the future of voting Josh really  a pleasure having you on today thank you  so much this has been fun

